{{- /* Angle define blocks (1..11). 由下方依 AssignedAngles 動態組裝，不複製成多份檔案。 */ -}}
{{- define "angle-1"}}
**Angle 1 — Line-by-line diff scan**
Read every hunk in the diff, then read the enclosing function for each hunk.
For every line ask: what input, state, timing, or platform makes this line wrong?
Look for: inverted/wrong conditions, off-by-one, nil/null deref, missing error check,
wrong-variable copy-paste, error swallowed, type mismatch, boundary values.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-2"}}
**Angle 2 — Removed-behavior audit**
For every line the diff DELETES or replaces, name the invariant it enforced.
Search the new code for where that invariant is re-established.
If you cannot find it, that is a candidate: a removed guard, a dropped error path,
a narrowed validation, a deleted test covering a real case.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-3"}}
**Angle 3 — Cross-file trace**
For each function the diff changes, find its callers (grep for the symbol) and check
whether the change breaks any call site: a new precondition, a changed return shape,
a new error, a timing/ordering dependency. Also check callees in the same diff.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-4"}}
**Angle 4 — Concurrency & state**
Race conditions, deadlocks, shared mutable state, missing locks, unsafe concurrent
map/slice access, goroutine leaks, missing context cancellation.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-5"}}
**Angle 5 — Reuse**
Flag new code that re-implements something the codebase already has.
Grep shared/utility modules and adjacent files. Name the existing helper.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-6"}}
**Angle 6 — Simplification**
Flag unnecessary complexity: redundant state, copy-paste with slight variation,
deep nesting, dead code left behind. Name the simpler form.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-7"}}
**Angle 7 — Altitude**
Check that each change is at the right depth. Special cases layered on shared
infrastructure suggest the fix is not deep enough — prefer generalizing the
underlying mechanism.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-8"}}
**Angle 8 — Convention compliance**
Read every "Included" section above (project CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or similar).
For each changed file, check whether the diff violates any convention stated there:
naming rules, comment requirements, error handling style, architectural boundaries,
forbidden patterns. Quote the specific rule being violated.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-9"}}
**Angle 9 — Git history regression check**
For each changed file, run `git log --oneline -20 -- <file>` and `git blame` on the
changed regions. Look for:
- Lines that were previously fixed (commit message says "fix"/"bugfix") being reverted or weakened
- Guards or validations added by past bug-fix commits being removed without justification
- Patterns that a past commit explicitly moved away from being reintroduced
Report the original fix commit hash and what invariant is at risk.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-10"}}
**Angle 10 — Code comment compliance**
For each changed file, scan ALL comments in the file (not just the diff) for:
- `// WARNING:`, `// IMPORTANT:`, `// NOTE:`, `// HACK:`, `// TODO:` markers
  that state constraints the diff may violate
- Comments that say "do not change X without updating Y" — verify Y was updated
- Stale comments: if the diff changes behavior, check that nearby comments still
  match the new behavior. Flag comments that describe the OLD behavior.
{{- end}}
{{- define "angle-11"}}
**Angle 11 — Past review feedback**
Check if prior rounds of this feature had review findings on the same files:
- Read previous round reports: {{.DotDir}}/{{.Feature.ID}}/rounds/round-*/review-report.md
  and deep-review-report.md (for rounds before {{.Round}})
- If a prior round flagged an issue in a file that this round also touches, verify
  the issue was actually fixed — not just worked around or reintroduced
- Flag any recurring pattern across rounds (same mistake in different files)
{{- end}}
